{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>For it is near</b> — and easy to return by that same road to Egypt. And there are many midrashic interpretations. This is the language of Rashi. And this is also the opinion of R. Abraham Ibn Ezra, for the reason of <b>\"And Elohim did not lead them through the land of the Philistines' road\"</b> is <b>\"because it is near,\"</b> and they would be discouraged and immediately return to Egypt. In my opinion, if it were as they say, <b>\"for Elohim said\"</b> would come first, and Scripture would say: <b>\"And Elohim did not lead them through the land of the Philistines' road, for Elohim said, because it is near, lest the people be discouraged.\"</b>\n\nBut the correct interpretation is that it says <b>\"And Elohim did not lead them through the land of the Philistines' road,\"</b> which is near and convenient for leading them on that road, <b>\"for Elohim said, lest the people be discouraged when they see war, and they return to Egypt.\"</b> The reason for the war is that they would have to pass through the land of the Philistines, and the Philistines would not let them pass in peace, and they would return to Egypt. But on the desert road they would see no war until they were in their land, in the land of Sihon and Og, kings of the Amorites, which is given to them, and they are far from Egypt at that time. And the war of Amalek at Rephidim was not reason enough to turn back on its account, for they were not passing through his territory — rather, he came from his land and fought against them out of his hatred for them, and if they resolved to return to Egypt it would not help, for he would fight them on the way, and also they were far from Egypt on the circuitous route they had traveled, and they knew no other road.\n\nThis is the language of Rashi on <b>\"when they see war\"</b>: for example, the war of the Canaanite and the Amalekite; had they gone on the straight road they would have turned back. How much more so — if when the road encircled them in a roundabout way they said, \"Let us resolve to return to Egypt\" — if He had led them on the direct route, how much more so? (Mekhilta). And this matter that it says <b>\"And Elohim did not lead them,\"</b> <b>\"but Elohim led the people around by the way of the wilderness\"</b> — when they set out from Succoth, the pillar of cloud began to go before them, and it did not go by way of the land of the Philistines, but went by way of the wilderness of Etham, and Israel followed after it. And the cloud came to rest at Etham, and they encamped there, and it is at the edge of the wilderness."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:07:37.654340593Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}